--- a/dm/games/0400486024DEC000/README.md
+++ b/dm/games/0400486024DEC000/README.md
@@ -125,6 +125,7 @@ From the same Kakariko save, walk into Hyrule Field and continue towards Castle
   - the threshold is the game's 83 (about 16.4 ms) at 60 fps. With the launcher's **Frame rate** at 30 (`DM_FPS_TARGET=30`) it scales by `60 / target` to 166 (about 32.8 ms), the GPU time a 30 fps frame has, so the resolution drops only when a frame would miss 30. On a MacBook Air M2 (10-core GPU, 16 GB, macOS 27.0, handheld 1440x810, MetalFX, 30 fps cap, scripted Kokiri Forest walk; [PR #1954](https://github.com/seedds/nxnative/pull/1954)), the game previously sat at its 854x480 floor; with the scaled threshold it stayed at level 0 (1440x810 in 100% of sampled frames, last load 134 < 166, frame p50 / p99 33.34 / 35.39 ms, 0.63% over 36 ms). Open: a heavy 30 fps scene that pushes load past 166 has not been measured yet;
   - down when the load has been over the threshold for 8 frames in a row, and that level is marked as not fitting;
   - up when the load fits and the level above was never measured, fitted when last measured, or was measured more than 600 frames ago (about 10 s at 60 fps, 20 s at 30).
+  - NXForge: **Resolution headroom** (`DM_DRS_HEADROOM`, 0 to 30 %, off by default) lowers the threshold the climb compares with by that share (state +0x44), so it settles below the edge. On an M4 Pro at full load the game sat at about 16.4 ms of 16.7 with a hold at 2 vsyncs about every 17 s (2026-11-11).
 
   Nothing is decided until a level has held 8 frames, since the load is read a few frames late. The 8 frames in a row keep a single slow frame, or the first frames at a new size, from rejecting a level that fits. An earlier version stepped down on 2 frames over and stopped at level 2 after one burst to 90 at level 1. A version that stepped up only when the load, scaled by the next level's pixel ratio, stayed under the threshold sat at the floor too: 70 × 1.3 is over 83. Result (M4 Pro, 2026-11-09, `kokiri-still`, the full library on chain a02f1e0dfdde): from the floor to full size, then mostly at 1536x864. The load there is about 83, the game's own threshold, so a sustained frame over moves it to level 1 (0.70) for about 10 s. 59.9 fps, all 1,800 frames held at one vsync, frame p99 16.89 ms, GPU p50 14.5 ms. Loads by level: 72 at the floor, 75 at level 6, 80 at level 2, 82 at level 1, 83 at full size.
 - Current: the launcher's **Render scale** (`DM_RENDER_SCALE`, 0.5 to 1.0, 1.0 by default) fixes the 3D views at that fraction of the game's own size and holds the dynamic resolution at its top level, as on Breath of the Wild and Tears of the Kingdom, with the game's own upscaler to the output. Two stubs in the view-size update (found by its fingerprint) multiply the scale the game computes and make the frame recompute the sizes whenever `[x20+0xe5c]` differs from the last scaled value; the level stub returns level 0 under a scale below 1.0 ([`patches.py`](patches.py), render scale; [PR #1903](https://github.com/seedds/nxnative/pull/1903)). Measured on an M4 Pro (20-core GPU, 2026-11-09, `kokiri-deck`, docked, two runs each, main `d8b2b64a1`, taken at graphics scale tier 1 where the top 3D size was 1536x864; the engine's own rounding gave 1171x659 at 0.75 and 781x439 at 0.5):
